  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 13,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.068,
            0.817,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.845,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "105"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.108,
            0.885,
            0.148
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you observe? The longer the chord, the closer it is to the centre. Let us try to understand why this is true."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.152,
            0.886,
            0.212
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theorem 8:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let AB and DE be two chords of a circle with centre C. Suppose "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "A B \\geq D E ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Then the distance from C to AB is less than the distance from C to DE."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.216,
            0.886,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As always, we draw the figure. Remember: by ‘distance from C to AB’ we mean the perpendicular distance. So, we drop perpendiculars CF and CG from C to AB and DE, respectively."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.294,
            0.414,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.144,
                0.294,
                0.414,
                0.483
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_13_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.244,
                0.491,
                0.323,
                0.51
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.16"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.453,
            0.294,
            0.632,
            0.314
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Given:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " AB > DE."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.32,
            0.706,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To show that:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " CF < CG."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.344,
            0.877,
            0.434
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why is this true?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " In Fig. 5.16, both AC and CD are radii. So, AC = CD. By the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras Theorem, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{CD^{2} = CG^{2} + GD^{2}}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{AC^{2} = CF^{2} + AF^{2}}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.428,
            0.737,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{CF^{2}+AF^{2}=CG^{2}+GD^{2}}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.454,
            0.876,
            0.513
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now AB is greater than DE. So AF is greater than GD (because F and G are the midpoints of AB and DE)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.516,
            0.828,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{AF^{2} \\geq GD^{2}}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " we have "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathsf { C F } ^ { 2 } \\leq \\mathsf { C G } ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " So, CF < CG."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.582,
            0.886,
            0.64
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Comment:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The chord nearest to the centre is the chord containing the centre. Its distance to C is zero! So the diameter is the greatest chord."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.64,
            0.886,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If one pushes the chord away from the centre, at some stage the chord becomes a single point, its length is zero, and the distance of C from this chord is the radius."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.733,
            0.622,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 5.5"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.78,
            0.885,
            0.823
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Find the length of the chord of a circle where the radius is 7 cm and perpendicular distance is 6 cm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.829,
            0.886,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Explain why the following statement is true: If the perpendicular distance of a chord from the centre is d and the radius is r, then the chord length is "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 \\sqrt { r ^ { 2 } - \\boldsymbol { d } ^ { 2 } }"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
